id: rust.tokio-watch
kind: rust
title: tokio::sync::watch
aliases: watch channel, latest value, config reload
tags: async, channels, configuration
questions:
  - how do you propagate a config change to every running task?
invariant: watch holds exactly one latest value; receivers observe changes and can always read the current value, so late readers stay correct.
answer_30s: watch is a single-slot broadcast for state that only has a "latest" version: config snapshots, feature flags, shutdown flags.
when_to_use:
  - config or flag distribution, latest-state publishers, shutdown flags
avoid_when:
  - every message must be processed (use broadcast or mpsc)
minimal_example: |
  use tokio::sync::watch;

  let (tx, mut rx) = watch::channel(Config::default());

  tokio::spawn(async move {
      while rx.changed().await.is_ok() {
          let cfg = rx.borrow().clone();
          tracing::info!(?cfg, "config reloaded");
      }
  });

  tx.send(new_config)?; // latest value wins
failure_modes:
  - using watch for events, so intermediate values are silently skipped
  - sending before any receiver exists, so the first update is missed
related:
  - rust.tokio-broadcast
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - design.cache-invalidation
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/sync/watch/index.html
